WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.320
Speaker 1: An anti-abortion, anti LGBT Christian&nbsp;
group, pulled the stunt to get themselves refused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:13.200
service at a restaurant. And now you've got a&nbsp;
lot of right wingers saying they were denied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.140
service for their religion. Is that actually&nbsp;
what happened? I want to start with this today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:25.860
because it is so important to understand when we&nbsp;
talk about nondiscrimination differences between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:32.880
beliefs and identity. And this is the perfect&nbsp;
example to talk about that. So let's start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:39.420
an article that tells us what happened. It's a CBS&nbsp;
News article. Restaurant Denies Christian Group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:49.440
Service Over Its Anti-abortion and LGBTQ stances.&nbsp;
Here's what happened. A restaurant in Richmond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.740
Virginia, refused to host a private event for&nbsp;
a conservative Christian organization over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:01.380
group's position on same sex marriage and abortion&nbsp;
rights. The restaurant is called Metzger Bar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:07.080
and Butchery, and it is known as an inclusive&nbsp;
establishment that has rarely refused service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:12.780
to willing patrons, but said it denied service&nbsp;
to the group in an effort to protect its staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.580
many of whom are members or member women&nbsp;
or members of the LGBTQ community. Quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.140
We recent recently we refuse service to a group&nbsp;
that had booked an event with us after the owners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.480
of Metzger found out it was a group of donors to a&nbsp;
political organization that seeks to deprive women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:33.180
and LGBTQ persons of their basic human rights in&nbsp;
Virginia. The move was consistent with Metzger's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.160
past practices, was made out of respect for&nbsp;
its staff. Quote We have always refuse service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.560
to anyone for making our staff uncomfortable or&nbsp;
unsafe. And this was the driving force behind our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:49.320
decision. All of our staff are people with rights&nbsp;
who deserve dignity and a safe work environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:57.660
This is a super interesting legal story because&nbsp;
discrimination along religious lines, meaning we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:05.580
don't serve Christians, would be against the law.&nbsp;
But political beliefs are not protected outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:14.340
of Washington, D.C.. And that is what is at the&nbsp;
crux of this. Now, I'm biased. My view here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:20.880
that nowhere is there any evidence that these&nbsp;
folks were denied service for being Christian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:28.560
They were denied service for their views on&nbsp;
particular issues, their view on abortion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:39.420
their view on LGBT rights. Now, where it starts&nbsp;
to get dicey is that as we have seen the religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:50.100
right start to behave over the last few years&nbsp;
is to argue, well, those views are informed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:55.980
our religion. And by telling us that those views&nbsp;
are what is getting us banned or refused service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.960
You are effectively discriminating against&nbsp;
our religion because there is no daylight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:08.160
between our view on abortion and LGBT rights&nbsp;
and our Christian faith. Now, the other part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.920
that's important to consider is that this was a&nbsp;
stunt. The Family Foundation is the name of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:20.220
group that did this. They obviously knew about&nbsp;
Metzger's history of supporting inclusivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:26.160
including LGBT inclusivity. They knew that they&nbsp;
tried to book an event there. And after they said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.020
no, they started soliciting donations because, oh,&nbsp;
we've been canceled. They've canceled us where?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.280
This was a very clear and known stunt and this&nbsp;
was the outcome. So there's a couple of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:42.720
things that I think are important to talk about.&nbsp;
From a legal standpoint, I can't adjudicate here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:50.820
whether based on all of the facts a court would&nbsp;
find saying no was because of Christianity or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:56.520
saying no was because of political views that&nbsp;
are not protected by nondiscrimination law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:03.480
The truth is, we all know there are progressive&nbsp;
Christian groups, progressive Catholic groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:10.260
etc.. Endless Jewish groups that have very&nbsp;
different views about abortion. There's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:16.140
rule that says if you are, quote, Christian,&nbsp;
you have to have this particular view about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:24.240
abortion or about LGBT people. So we know that&nbsp;
you have other perspectives there. And so.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:32.940
It's obvious that if this were a progressive&nbsp;
Christian group or a Christian group that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:38.580
had a let others decide view, we don't like&nbsp;
it, but let others decide about abortion or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:43.500
whatever the case may be. They would not have&nbsp;
been refused service. And so by definition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:50.100
that is not religiously based discrimination.&nbsp;
The problem is that thanks to what happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:56.520
the judiciary in the United States under Donald&nbsp;
Trump, one of the biggest victories that genuinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:04.620
took place during the Trump administration was the&nbsp;
ability of Donald Trump and the success for Donald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:10.440
Trump of moving the judiciary dramatically to the&nbsp;
right in the United States. Now, most prominently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.680
we think about the Supreme Court, and of&nbsp;
course, that is prominent. And that's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.640
most of us know about long public confirmation&nbsp;
hearings. We know the names of these folks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:26.400
etc.. But at many other levels, the appointment&nbsp;
of right wing judges by Donald Trump has led to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:34.560
judiciary that is much more likely to find. Yeah,&nbsp;
even though really they say it's the views, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:41.460
the religion. You can't separate those religious&nbsp;
views and this is effectively, religiously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:46.380
based discrimination. You must host the Family&nbsp;
Foundation. There are judges in the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:52.200
that would find that, and that's very, very scary.&nbsp;
Now, at the back or the bottom or the top of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:57.720
however you want to describe it. There's always&nbsp;
the hypocrisy with these people. They want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:05.280
have it both ways. On the one hand, they want to&nbsp;
be able to discriminate against the LGBT people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:11.460
whether it's the anti-gay baker or whatever story&nbsp;
you want to look at. They want that freedom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:22.020
but they also want everyone else to have to accept&nbsp;
and suck up and allow their bigotry and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:28.200
discrimination and their whatever it may be in the&nbsp;
particular circumstance, xenophobia or homophobia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.640
or whatever the case may be. And then they&nbsp;
frame it as religious when they want to do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:40.800
discrimination. It's their right because of their&nbsp;
religion. And then when they want to demand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:48.360
they be serviced, they say it's discrimination not&nbsp;
to serve us. They want it both ways. There is not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:54.540
A lot of exciting stuff happening in hospitality&nbsp;
from the from the from this particular perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.540 --> 00:07:01.694
right now. But the right to refuse service is a&nbsp;
real thing. And as long as you're not violating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.694 --> 00:07:08.100
nondiscrimination law, which includes religion,&nbsp;
etc., then you should be allowed to do it. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:12.300
believe that the restaurant was well within its&nbsp;
rights to say because of your views they didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:17.760
even mention their religion because of your views.&nbsp;
We are not going to offer you service. We will see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.960
what it leads to in terms of lawsuits and if&nbsp;
there is an outcome, then we will get to that

